  "claim": "The Abbey of Saint-Étienne was founded in 1545 by a descendant of Rollo.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen. Sentence: The Abbey of Saint-Étienne, also known as Abbaye aux Hommes (\"Men's Abbey\") by contrast with the Abbaye aux Dames (\"Ladies' Abbey\"), is a former Benedictine monastery in the French city of Caen, Normandy, dedicated to Saint Stephen.\nPage: Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen. Sentence: It was founded in 1063 by William the Conqueror and is one of the most important Romanesque buildings in Normandy.\nPage: William the Conqueror. Sentence: William I (c. 1028 – 9 September 1087), usually known as William the Conqueror and sometimes William the Bastard, was the first Norman King of England, reigning from 1066 until his death in 1087.\nPage: William the Conqueror. Sentence: A descendant of Rollo, he was Duke of Normandy (as Duke William II) from 1035 onward.",
  "evidence_text": "Page: Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen. Sentence: The Abbey of Saint-Étienne, also known as Abbaye aux Hommes (\"Men's Abbey\") by contrast with the Abbaye aux Dames (\"Ladies' Abbey\"), is a former Benedictine monastery in the French city of Caen, Normandy, dedicated to Saint Stephen.\nPage: Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen. Sentence: It was founded in 1063 by William the Conqueror and is one of the most important Romanesque buildings in Normandy.\nPage: William the Conqueror. Sentence: William I (c. 1028 – 9 September 1087), usually known as William the Conqueror and sometimes William the Bastard, was the first Norman King of England, reigning from 1066 until his death in 1087.\nPage: William the Conqueror. Sentence: A descendant of Rollo, he was Duke of Normandy (as Duke William II) from 1035 onward.",
