      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "06eb24bb-ca20-5b52-88ad-027c9757695f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Caen, Normandy, England\n- Organizations: Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Abbaye aux Hommes, Abbaye aux Dames\n- People: William the Conqueror, Rollo\n- Roles: Norman King of England, Duke of Normandy\n- Concepts: Benedictine monastery, Romanesque architecture\n\nFacts:\n- William the Conqueror, also called William I and sometimes William the Bastard, was born around 1028 and was a descendant of Rollo.\n- William the Conqueror became Duke of Normandy as Duke William II in 1035.\n- The Abbey of Saint-Étienne in Caen, Normandy, was founded in 1063 by William the Conqueror.\n- William the Conqueror was the first Norman King of England from 1066 until his death on 9 September 1087.\n- The Abbey of Saint-Étienne, also called Abbaye aux Hommes, is a former Benedictine monastery in the French city of Caen dedicated to Saint Stephen.\n- The Abbey of Saint-Étienne is contrasted with the Abbaye aux Dames and is one of the most important Romanesque buildings in Normandy.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8814c812-0276-5ab2-8e1c-6e66345511c3",
        "text": "Page: Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen. Sentence: The Abbey of Saint-Étienne, also known as Abbaye aux Hommes (\"Men's Abbey\") by contrast with the Abbaye aux Dames (\"Ladies' Abbey\"), is a former Benedictine monastery in the French city of Caen, Normandy, dedicated to Saint Stephen.\nPage: Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen. Sentence: It was founded in 1063 by William the Conqueror and is one of the most important Romanesque buildings in Normandy.\nPage: William the Conqueror. Sentence: William I (c. 1028 – 9 September 1087), usually known as William the Conqueror and sometimes William the Bastard, was the first Norman King of England, reigning from 1066 until his death in 1087.\nPage: William the Conqueror. Sentence: A descendant of Rollo, he was Duke of Normandy (as Duke William II) from 1035 onward.",
